### 6. JEST SETUP FILES

**`D:\code\ESI.ts\src\config\jest\jest.setup.ts`** -- this is the most important setup file:
```typescript
import { ApiClientBuilder } from '../../core/ApiClientBuilder';
import { RateLimiter } from '../../core/rateLimiter/RateLimiter';
import { getConfig } from '../../config/configManager';
import fetchMock from 'jest-fetch-mock';
import { getBody } from '../../../src/core/util/testHelpers';
import '../../../types/global.d.ts';

fetchMock.enableMocks();

const config = getConfig();
const rateLimiter = new RateLimiter();
rateLimiter.setTestMode(true);

const client = new ApiClientBuilder()
  .setClientId(config.projectName)
  .setLink(config.link)
  .setAccessToken(process.env.ESI_ACCESS_TOKEN || 'test-token')
  .setRateLimiter(rateLimiter)
  .build();

export const getClient = () => client;

beforeEach(() => {
  fetchMock.resetMocks();
  rateLimiter.reset();
});

global.getBody = getBody;
```

Key behaviors:
- Enables `jest-fetch-mock` globally so all `fetch` calls are intercepted
- Creates a shared client with rate limiter in test mode
- Exports `getClient()` for any test that needs the pre-configured client
- Resets fetch mocks and rate limiter state before every test
- Sets `getBody` as a global function available in all tests

**`D:\code\ESI.ts\src\core\util\testHelpers.ts`:**
```typescript
export const getBody = async (apiCall: () => Promise<any>): Promise<any> => {
  return apiCall();
};
```
This is a thin wrapper -- it simply invokes the passed async function. It exists to provide a uniform pattern for extracting API response bodies across all tests.

**`D:\code\ESI.ts\types\global.d.ts`:** Extends `NodeJS.Global` and `globalThis` to add the `getBody` type declaration.

**`D:\code\ESI.ts\src\config\jest\globalSetup.ts`** and **`globalTeardown.ts`:** Minimal -- just log messages.

